    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.diff\n\n### Description\n\nI'm Using slightly modification of the `MiniDiffUpdated` example from the docs:\nhttps://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.diff/blob/a2df2a9a485328c20252b7cadb11feb717020e45/doc/mini-diff.txt#L218\n\nlike so:\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"User\", {\n\tpattern = \"MiniDiffUpdated\",\n\tcallback = function(data)\n\t\tlocal s = vim.b[data.buf].minidiff_summary\n\t\tlocal t = {}\n\t\tif s.add > 0 then table.insert(t, \" +\" .. s.add) end\n\t\tif s.change > 0 then table.insert(t, \" ~\" .. s.change) end\n\t\tif s.delete > 0 then table.insert(t, \" -\" .. s.delete) end\n\t\tvim.b[data.buf].minidiff_summary_string = table.concat(t)\n\tend\n})\n```\nI typically have a tmux split running, with nvim in one and a normal CLI in the other. I use that other CLI generic commands like my git operations. \n\nWhen I run an interactive rebase, e.g.: `git rebase -i HEAD~3`, stop to amend some of the commits in between an move forward with `git rebase --continue` I encounter nil errors like the one below.\n\nError in User Autocommands for \"MiniDiffUpdated\":\n```\nLua callback: /home/matt/development/dotfiles/.config/nvim/init.lua:203: attempt to compare number with nil\nstack traceback:\n        /home/matt/development/dotfiles/.config/nvim/init.lua:203: in function </home/matt/development/dotfiles/.config/nvim/init.lu\na:199>\n        [C]: in function 'nvim_exec_autocmds'\n        ...hare/nvim/site/pack/core/opt/mini.nvim/lua/mini/diff.lua:1315: in function ''\n        vim/_editor.lua: in function <vim/_editor.lua:0>\n```\n\nI could adjust this on my end by adding the proper type and `nil` checks, but I also noticed similar errors where `s `, `s.add`, `s.change` and `s.delete`would also be `nil`. Perhaps `mini.diff` could ensure that `s` is properly initialized with e.g. `add`, `change` and `delete` `= 0` and so on? Or at least the doc update?\n",
